  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 7560214a2c335bd4e9cc29951d82c1e3, type: 3}
  m_Name: Damaso
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::NPCDialogueSO
  npcID: fray_damaso
  displayName: Fray Damaso
  characterFullName: "D\xE1maso Verdolagas"
  alias: "Padre D\xE1maso"
  characterCategory: Antagonist / Clergy / Franciscan Friar
  portrait: {fileID: 21300000, guid: dfb512c37edbc1a4186f758a9558d61e, type: 3}
  description: A loud, forceful and domineering Franciscan friar who formerly served
    as the parish priest of San Diego. He speaks with great confidence and shows
    open contempt toward Filipinos and government interference in religious authority.
  biography: "Fray D\xE1maso is a Franciscan friar who claims to have lived in the
    Philippines for twenty-three years. He served in San Diego for twenty years and
    knew many of its residents through his position as their curate and confessor.
    During Capit\xE1n Tiago\u2019s gathering, he argues that Filipinos are naturally
    ignorant and indolent and condemns reforms introduced by the colonial government.
    His hostility becomes especially clear when the discussion turns to the burial
    of Don Rafael Ibarra and when he treats Cris\xF3stomo Ibarra with contempt."
  characterFacts:
  - He belongs to the Franciscan order.
  - He served as the curate of San Diego for twenty years.
  - He claims to have spent twenty-three years in the Philippines.
  - "He was transferred from San Diego after the controversy involving Don Rafael\u2019s
    remains."
  - He speaks loudly and frequently interrupts other people.
  - He strongly defends the authority of the friars.
  - He expresses prejudiced views about Filipinos.
  - He denies having been an intimate friend of Don Rafael.
  introductionLines:
  - I am Fray Damaso, a Franciscan and former curate of San Diego.
  - After more than twenty years in this country, I know its people well.
  repeatDialogues:
  - lines:
    - Do not come to me with theories and fine speeches
    - Experience has taught me more than any book could.
